# Environment for the two-tier dev stack (deploy/docker-compose.dev.yml).
#
#   cp deploy/.env.dev.example deploy/.env.dev
#   task up-dev
#   task tier-dev
#
# The whole point of this stack is one licensed container next to one unlicensed
# one. A missing or unverifiable LICENSE_KEY does not stop anything: the
# enterprise container starts, logs a warning and serves as community, leaving
# two identical containers that look healthy and prove nothing. `task tier-dev`
# exists to catch exactly that, so run it before trusting the stack.
#
# deploy/.env.dev is gitignored. Keep it that way — the storage keys below are
# real, and LICENSE_KEY is a credential.

# --- Enterprise licence ---
# Only service-enterprise receives these. service-community deliberately gets
# nothing, which is what makes the two comparable.
#
# The verification keys, as "<kid>:<hex>" pairs separated by commas. The kid in
# the token's footer picks one, so a signing key can be rotated by listing two.
# The pair below is the key easyp-tech signs with; it is public by design and
# also ships in deploy/charts/easyp-service/values.yaml.
LICENSE_PUBLIC_KEYS=2026-08:81322461987167d5cfd529e9cb8b96f4797f12fce6be4399a0866e250c9b6bb5
# The token itself is a credential and is deliberately absent here. Take it from
# the licence registry (easyp-tech/licenses) under issued/<customer>/, which is
# also the only place it can be issued — this repository can verify tokens but
# not sign them. Check one before pasting it:
#
#   cd ../licenses && ./easyp-license verify --file issued/easyp-tech/<date>.token
#
# The token must carry iss=easyp.tech, aud=easyp-service, nbf, exp and
# tier=enterprise; any missing claim silently downgrades the container to
# community. Note that max_workers and max_plugins in a token are ignored —
# the tier alone decides the limits, which for enterprise means unlimited.
LICENSE_KEY=

# --- Write credentials ---
# The tier configs ship no write tokens: this stack is the one
# docker-compose.public.yml puts on the internet, and a committed digest is a
# published one. Until this is set, CreatePlugin, UpdatePlugin and DeletePlugin
# reject every call — which is the right state for a host a stranger can reach,
# but it does mean `easyp-svc plugins register` against this stack needs it.
#
# Digests only, never the tokens: "ci=<64 hex>,me=<64 hex>".
# Generate a pair with: easyp-svc auth new-token --name ci
AUTH_WRITE_TOKENS=

# --- Plugin storage ---
# Both tiers read plugin archives from the S3 endpoint named in
# deploy/config/config.{community,enterprise}.dev.yml. The endpoint, bucket and
# region live there because they are not secret; the key pair lives here because
# it is. Fill these in before `task up-dev` or plugin downloads fail with an
# authorization error from the store.
REGISTRY_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
REGISTRY_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# --- Public hostname ---
# Names the hosts docker-compose.public.yml certifies and routes: the routers
# answer to community.$EASYP_DEV_DOMAIN, enterprise.$EASYP_DEV_DOMAIN and the
# bare domain. Required by that overlay — it refuses to start without it — and
# unused by the plain dev stack, which answers on *.easyp.localhost.
# EASYP_DEV_DOMAIN=plugins.beta.easyp.tech

# --- Telemetry endpoints ---
# The tier configs leave these empty because docker-compose.dev.yml brings up no
# collector. Set them when layering docker-compose.observability.yml on top;
# without it the exporters retry a name that does not resolve and fill the log.
# TELEMETRY_OTLP_ENDPOINT=easyp-alloy:4317
# TELEMETRY_PYROSCOPE_ENDPOINT=http://easyp-pyroscope:4040

# --- Where the stack listens ---
# Everything except traefik binds to loopback. The database password above the
# service configs is committed in a public repository, so a stack brought up on
# a machine with a public address hands the database to anyone who reads the
# file — which is how the dev host came to have 5432 open to the internet.
# Set this to 0.0.0.0 only on a machine you know is not reachable from one.
# EASYP_BIND=127.0.0.1

# --- Host UID/GID ---
# The image runs as 65532 and the plugin caches are bind mounts, so on Linux the
# container cannot write to them unless this matches the directory's owner.
# Docker Desktop remaps ownership and does not need it.
# EASYP_UID=1000
# EASYP_GID=1000

# --- Service image ---
# Which published image the two tier containers run. A release tag (v1.0.0),
# `edge` for the tip of master, or `sha-<short>` to pin one commit. Bump this and
# run `docker compose pull && docker compose up -d` to upgrade.
#
# Not `latest`: it is published only on a release tag, so it silently means
# "the last release", and compose will happily keep a cached layer from months
# ago. A config file in this repository and the binary that reads it have to
# agree about which defaults exist.
# EASYP_SERVICE_VERSION=v1.0.0

# --- Observability (docker-compose.observability.yml) ---
# The telemetry backends write to an object store. These may point at the same
# store as the plugin archives — the buckets are prefixed so the two cannot be
# confused — or at a separate one, which is better if you can get a key for it.
# OBS_S3_URL=https://storage.example.com
# OBS_S3_ENDPOINT=storage.example.com
# OBS_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# OBS_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# Grafana is reachable from the internet on this stack, so it will not start
# without a password. Its own default is admin/admin.
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# The telemetry buckets, named for the environment they belong to. The default
# is the dev one, so a stack brought up without this cannot write where
# production data lives. Set it to easyp-prod-telemetry- for production, which
# is also where a separate key for those buckets would go.
# OBS_BUCKET_PREFIX=easyp-dev-telemetry-
